WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.070
I realize I'm stumbling as I'm speaking.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.600
So as a result of speaking with
Sohra every day, I decided that I

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.060
should go back and review the verbs.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.700
Hi there, Steve Kaufman here again.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.940
And, uh, I, first of all,
I want to review my week.

00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:19.515
Uh, my...

00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:23.925
I'm in this, uh, you know, a Streak with
Steve challenge and I'm trying to maintain

00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:26.715
my LingQ streak and Arabic and Persian.

00:00:26.744 --> 00:00:29.354
So I want to update you on my
activities for the past week.

00:00:29.655 --> 00:00:33.315
Remember if you, uh, enjoy
these videos, please subscribe,

00:00:33.885 --> 00:00:35.735
uh, click on the bell for...

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.095
for notifications.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.520
Um, you know, I'm, I'm hoping, and I
think the reaction I get from any of

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.450
my viewers is that, uh, my videos doing
encourage them in language learning.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:48.090
I think it's very important
for people to be encouraged.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.650
I think the most important role that
anyone involved in language teaching

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.580
or language instruction, the most
important role one can have is to,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:04.125
is to trigger curiosity, enthusiasm,
hunger, the desire to learn.

00:01:04.155 --> 00:01:08.054
That's the absolute key, more
important than any explanations or

00:01:08.384 --> 00:01:12.945
techniques of learning or anything
of those, uh, of that kind it's to

00:01:12.945 --> 00:01:18.134
get people motivated to trigger their
enthusiasm, their hunger for learning.

00:01:18.705 --> 00:01:25.755
So I had my week, uh, in Persian in Farsi
in Persian, and every day I spoke with my

00:01:25.755 --> 00:01:35.205
tutor Sahra in Iran who is in the town of
Rasht in Gilan province, in Northern Iran.

00:01:35.205 --> 00:01:39.855
And she showed me some pictures from
there and it looks, semi-tropical quite

00:01:39.915 --> 00:01:45.435
unlike the image that we normally have
of Iran is sort of a very dry climate.

00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:51.134
And, um, I certainly feel that I spoke
a lot better at the end of the week

00:01:51.134 --> 00:01:53.085
than I did at the beginning of the week.

00:01:53.505 --> 00:02:00.134
I try to start my day with 13,
my obligatory, obligatory sort of

00:02:00.134 --> 00:02:05.205
minimal goal of 13 LingQs created
in each of Arabic and Persian.

00:02:05.205 --> 00:02:05.914
So I do that.

00:02:05.935 --> 00:02:06.585
Get that out of the way.

00:02:07.565 --> 00:02:08.854
That's my sort of duty.

00:02:09.544 --> 00:02:14.385
Uh, and I did, uh, again, a lot of
listening and reading, but in particular

00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:19.035
as a result of, of, uh, talking
to Sahra every day for an hour, of

00:02:19.035 --> 00:02:20.925
course, I realized where my gaps are.

00:02:21.315 --> 00:02:24.105
And the biggest problem
I have is with verbs.

00:02:24.135 --> 00:02:27.915
Of course, I'm missing a lot of
vocabulary, but I like I'll, you

00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:29.595
know, acquire those over time.

00:02:30.165 --> 00:02:35.085
Uh, I'm often missing some of the
most basic items of vocabulary, which

00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:36.555
I've forgotten, which I once knew.

00:02:37.125 --> 00:02:41.910
And, uh, as I said, in one of my
tweets, I'm always impressed with how

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.550
easy it is to forget even the most
basic, you know, words in the language.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.510
And yet here we are, I'm speaking
with Sahra, we have a conversation.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:56.730
So some words do stick, but some words
when I need them, I can't find them.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.500
That's absolutely normal.

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.850
And, but what I find is
that verbs give me trouble.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.280
And I think that's true
in a lot of languages.

00:03:05.579 --> 00:03:10.260
That's why our mini stories at LingQ
focus on the most, you know, common

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:14.970
verbs in the language, the highest
frequency verbs for comprehension.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.109
Of course, we're always going to miss some
key nouns, but when we go to speak, I find

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:24.390
that if I can't do well on the verbs, I
have a lot of trouble expressing myself.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.290
And when I was speaking with
her, I realized that I was.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.560
For example, in, in Persian,
there's a subjunctive.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.510
And when do you actually
use the subjunctive?

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:38.280
And, and, uh, there are different
tenses, uh, and, and in the spoken form

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.840
of the language, there's no future.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.230
So it seems strange that we're using
the present tense for the future tense.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:47.400
And there are many things like that that
I realize I'm stumbling as I'm speaking.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:54.950
So as a result of speaking with
Sahra every day, I decided that I

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.410
should go back and review the verbs.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:01.980
Which, and I've read this book many times
and you know, it just has a hundred,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.670
the most, you probably can't see these
too well but the highest frequency

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.420
verbs in all their different forms.

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:09.190
And I just kind of leaf through it.

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:11.850
I go jump around, look up different verbs.

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:16.709
The other thing I did was I did a
lot of listening to the mini stories

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:21.825
where I know, you know, I wouldn't
say all, but a lot of the vocabulary,

00:04:22.005 --> 00:04:25.635
but the mini stories are based on
the most frequently used verbs.

00:04:25.845 --> 00:04:30.435
And there, you know, the story, you might
have one story in one tense and another

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:32.385
story in another tense or another person.

00:04:32.385 --> 00:04:36.195
So this is a great way
to review the verbs.

00:04:36.555 --> 00:04:39.615
And I think this is again, an
important point, you know, I've

00:04:39.615 --> 00:04:43.725
often said in some of my other videos
that you can't master the basics.

00:04:44.145 --> 00:04:47.115
We're constantly looping
back to do the basics.

00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:50.685
Again, I've listened to the
mini stories, 30, 40, 50 times.

00:04:50.835 --> 00:04:53.625
And I discover something
new every time I listen.

00:04:53.865 --> 00:04:59.315
So to me, language learning as a matter
of constantly looping back, we push

00:04:59.325 --> 00:05:05.105
forward into new content, uh, in order
to quiet, acquire more vocabulary, but at

00:05:05.105 --> 00:05:06.905
the same and improve our comprehension.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.289
But at the same time, in order to
be able to talk, we have to be able

00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:12.270
to use the words, the verbs rather.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.799
And so I go back to the mini stories
and there's this constant looping

00:05:16.799 --> 00:05:18.539
back and then charging forward.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.820
And of course it helps to have a
great tutor that, you know, triggers

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:25.740
that enthusiasm, that hunger,

00:05:26.235 --> 00:05:30.355
uh, gets you going, uh, which
is the case with Sahara.

00:05:30.375 --> 00:05:33.405
And I never have to think of what I'm
going to say because she constantly

00:05:33.405 --> 00:05:35.325
asks me questions, keeps me going.

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:36.105
I don't have to worry.

00:05:36.255 --> 00:05:37.335
What are we going to talk about?

00:05:37.425 --> 00:05:39.045
That's her problem, not my problem.

00:05:39.195 --> 00:05:43.335
And I know that she will get me
talking and she's a wonderful

00:05:43.335 --> 00:05:47.505
tutor because she not only has
created content for us at LingQ,

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:53.120
uh, on the history of Iran, on the cuisine
of Iran on so many different things.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.610
Right now, she's done one on the famous
Iranian movie directors, and she's even

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:05.600
found, she sent me a 30-minute sound
file from one of the famous Mahmud

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.830
Farhadi is a famous director and he
has this movie called The Salesman.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.890
Um, Forushande.

00:06:16.094 --> 00:06:16.755
I don't remember.

00:06:17.265 --> 00:06:22.034
Uh, I don't worry about what I can
remember and Forushande I think it is,

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:26.565
but, but so then the sound file is so
full of car noises and doors slamming

00:06:26.565 --> 00:06:32.025
and other miscellaneous noises that
it's not great listening all by itself

00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:34.544
because it's not sufficiently word rich.

00:06:34.544 --> 00:06:36.255
And I think this is often
the case with movies.

00:06:36.525 --> 00:06:38.025
Movies again, stimulate you.

00:06:38.025 --> 00:06:39.195
They create that hunger.

00:06:39.195 --> 00:06:40.305
You want to learn the language.

00:06:40.845 --> 00:06:46.515
Uh, I'm enjoying watching Danish, you
know, Badehotellet, but in terms of really

00:06:46.515 --> 00:06:52.035
working on either verbs or increasing
your vocabulary, uh, I find that, uh,

00:06:52.095 --> 00:06:56.505
audio content that's richer, denser
in terms of words is more effective.

00:06:56.715 --> 00:06:58.485
Anyway, that's what I've been doing.

00:06:58.515 --> 00:07:02.145
So I wanted to update you on
my week two of my challenge.

00:07:02.475 --> 00:07:04.695
I think I'm keeping up with my streak.

00:07:05.035 --> 00:07:08.145
It's a low bar, 13
LingQs created every day.

00:07:08.235 --> 00:07:11.475
Uh, typically I'm creating, if it's
the language that I'm on for that

00:07:11.475 --> 00:07:16.875
week, I'm creating, you know, 50, 60 at
least, uh, next week I'm into Arabic.

00:07:17.505 --> 00:07:21.375
Uh, and I'm hoping to be
talking every day with Mohammed.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:26.220
I sometimes get very kind offers
from people who say they'll help

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:27.960
me in my Persian or my Arabic.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.460
Uh, right now I'm kind of committed
to those tutors and don't have a lot

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.520
of time to get involved with other
people in those languages cause

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.900
I'd also have other things to do.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.070
The last two days I played golf with,
with my wife and we did a lot of cleaning

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.350
up after our renovations in the kitchen.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:47.940
So there's always lots of stuff to do, but
I have my routine in Persian and Arabic

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.160
and that's what I'm kind of sticking to.

